{"code": "el", "name": "Greek"}, "word": "στήθος"}, {"language": {"code": "ckb", "name": "Central Kurdish"}, "word": "سنگ"}, {"language": {"code": "kmr", "name": "Northern Kurdish"}, "word": "bersîng"}, {"language": {"code": "nb", "name": "Norwegian Bokmål"}, "word": "bryst"}, {"language": {"code": "ru", "name": "Russian"}, "word": "грудь"}, {"language": {"code": "gd", "name": "Scottish Gaelic"}, "word": "broilleach"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The figurative seat of the emotions, feelings etc.; one’s heart or innermost thoughts.", "tags": [], "examples": ["She kindled hope in the breast of all who heard her."], "quotes": [{"text": "He has a loyal breast.", "reference": "1613 (date written), William Shakespeare, [John Fletcher], “The Famous History of the Life of King Henry the Eight”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene ii]:"}, {"text": "[…]Thou best know’st\nWhat torment I did find thee in. Thy groans\nDid make wolves howl, and penetrate the breasts\nOf ever-angry bears—it was a torment\nTo lay upon the damn’d, which Sycorax\nCould not again undo. It was mine art,\nWhen I arrived and heard thee, that made gape\nThe pine and let thee out.", "reference": "1610–1611 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tempest”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene ii]:"}, {"text": "Muſick has Charms to ſooth a ſavage Breaſt, / To ſoften Rocks, or bend a knotted Oak.", "reference": "1697, [William] Congreve, The Mourning Bride, a Tragedy. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC, Act I, page 1:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The ventral portion of an animal’s thorax.", "tags": [], "examples": ["The robin has a red breast."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "word": "դոշ"}, {"language": {"code": "zh", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "胸甲"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "borst"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "rinta"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "poitrine"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "poitrail"}, {"language": {"code": "el", "name": "Greek"}, "word": "στήθος"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "mell"}, {"language": {"code": "is", "name": "Icelandic"}, "word": "bringa"}, {"language": {"code": "ga", "name": "Irish"}, "word": "ucht"}, {"language": {"code": "kmr", "name": "Northern Kurdish"}, "word": "sîng"}, {"language": {"code": "kmr", "name": "Northern Kurdish"}, "word": "bersîng"}, {"language": {"code": "kmr", "name": "Northern Kurdish"}, "word": "berçîsk"}, {"language": {"code": "mk", "name": "Macedonian"}, "word": "гради"}, {"language": {"code": "nb", "name": "Norwegian Bokmål"}, "word": "bryst"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "Portuguese"}, "word": "peito"}, {"language": {"code": "ru", "name": "Russian"}, "word": "грудь"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "pechuga"}, {"language": {"code": "sv", "name": "Swedish"}, "word": "bröst"}, {"language": {"code": "ta", "name": "Tamil"}, "word": "மார்பு"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A choice cut of poultry, especially chicken or turkey, taken from the bird’s breast; also a cut of meat from other animals, breast of mutton, veal, pork.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Would you like breast or wing?"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The front or forward part of anything.", "tags": [], "examples": ["chimney breast", "plough breast"], "quotes": [{"text": "If you burn wood on its own, the flue gases are cooler than from a coal fire. This can result in tars condensing out within the parging and brickwork of the flue, sometimes causing brown or yellow staining on upstairs chimney breasts.", "reference": "2015 April 7, Jeff Howell, “The secret of longer lasting tiles [print version: How to avoid cracking up, 4 April 2015, p. P7]”, in The Daily Telegraph (Property), archived from the original on 22 Apr 2015:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "The upper surface of a landform or body of water.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mountains on whose barren breast / The labouring clouds do often rest.", "reference": "a. 1645, John Milton, “L’Allegro”, in Poems of Mr. John Milton, […], London: […] Ruth Raworth for Humphrey Mosely, […], published 1646, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "[…] In the distance the flowing glaze, the breast of the river, with a wind-dapple here and there, […]", "reference": "1865, Walt Whitman, “When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom’d”, in Sequel to Drum-Taps: When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom’d and other poems:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(mining) The face of a coal working.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(mining) The front of a furnace.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) The power of singing; a musical voice.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "By my troth, the fool has an excellent breast.", "reference": "c. 1601–1602 (date written), William Shakespeare, “Twelfe Night, or What You Will”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene iii]:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(swimming) The breaststroke.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["chest", "heart", "soul", "white meat", "brisket"], "antonyms": ["thigh", "wing", "dark meat"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɹɛst/", "tags": ["General American", "Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɹest/", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɾɛst/", "tags": ["Scotland", "Wales"]}], "forms": [{"word": "breasts", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "breasting", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "breasted", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "breasted", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "brest", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive, often figurative) To push against with the breast; to meet full on, oppose, face.", "tags": ["figuratively", "often", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[…]when the court, very much to the credit of their candour and firmness, breasted the popular current by sustaining the demurrer.", "reference": "1817, William Wirt, Sketches of the Life and Character of Patrick Henry, page 22:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "противопоставям се"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "ponnistella"}, {"language": {"code": "kmr", "name": "Northern Kurdish"}, "word": "tê koşîn"}, {"language": {"code": "kmr", "name": "Northern Kurdish"}, "word": "hewl dan"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To reach the top of (a hill or other prominence).", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["He breasted the hill and saw the town before him."], "quotes": [{"text": "Vera Nugent, a brisk woman of the world, with a fondness for vivid clothing and a Spanish air which went oddly with it, took the trouble one fine day to tackle her brother. “Look here, Jimmy,\" she said as they breasted a mountain pass, “are you quite sure what you are up to with these people?”", "reference": "1916, Maurice Hewlett, Love and Lucy, page 247:"}, {"text": "With the sea below us grey and storm swept, and the coast line vague in the driving rain, we forged our way up the bank to breast the summit at 37 m.p.h.", "reference": "1947 January and February, O. S. Nock, “\"The Aberdonian\" in Wartime”, in Railway Magazine, page 8:"}, {"text": "But this was excelled by the remarkable feat of Iron Duke, on the second run, in accelerating from a dead start at Miller's Dale up 1 in 90 to no less than 53 m.p.h. before breasting the summit at Peak Forest.", "reference": "1960 March, Cecil J. Allen, “Locomotive Running Past and Present”, in Trains Illustrated, page 178:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, cooking) To debreast.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Breast the birds; wash and dry well. Preheat oven to 375 degrees. Place the birds in a roasting pan.", "reference": "2005, Texas Judicial Cookbook: Hello There!:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breast", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}